  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "note-sheet",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "The scoreboard sheet\n\nRound three, on the sheet. The list is flat: every pilot in one list ranked by kills, their side in the Team column and in the color of their name. Time is gone. A press on a pilot (enter, click or tap) opens their card, a panel that stacked, and the card's breathing key is JOIN, named for their side. So a side is joined through one of its pilots, the way an invitation was sent from the info box, and the selectable section round two needed is not needed.\n\nThe stop stays: SCOREBOARD, answering where you stand. TEAMS is drawn beside it on the Team Battle board to compare.\n\nWhat this costs: a side with nobody on it has no row to press, so it cannot be joined from here; in Free Roam, founding a side needs a door of its own."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-zones",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "By zone\n\nOne sheet, five zones. The line under the head says the sides, with each side's stands or flags in Turf and War, the ones nobody holds dim. The duel's two pilots are the two rows, with a Rounds band under them listing who took each and how long it ran. Free Roam is one ranked list of thirty one with the Team column doing the work the colors cannot for eight sides, scrolling under the pinned head."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-band",
      "x": -470,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 400,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "The band\n\nThe sheet hangs off an instrument, and the instrument can be one of three: the shipped 26-point band at top center, a 16-point line in the same place, or a stack in the top left corner. The big sheet draws all three for every zone; the boards under it put each in a Turf room on a monitor and a War room on a phone, with the corner band once with the sheet up.\n\nMiddle keeps the first glance. Thin spends less of the top edge and makes the clock small. Corner clears the top edge entirely and sits where the room's chips come and go."
    }
  ],
